                    coach_r1_sic_right:     `<strong>Right read on the SiC IP.</strong> It is strong but adjacent and under-exploited — the canonical asset to leverage out, not lock away. Two years ahead of rivals and earning ~€0 is value sitting idle.`,
                    coach_r1_sic_hoard:     `<strong>Locking the SiC IP away “to be safe”.</strong> It has earned roughly €0 for years and the patents keep ageing. Hoarding an idle asset has a real cost — when does protecting it become just wasting it?`,
                    coach_r1_sic_lapse:     `<strong>Letting the patents lapse throws away a two-year lead.</strong> The SiC architecture is not worthless — a tier-1 will pay €9M for it. Peripheral is the wrong box; this is an asset to leverage, not bin.`,
                    coach_r1_build_outsource: `<strong>Outsourcing the whole 800V platform hands away your core.</strong> Power-electronics control is central to your advantage — keep it closed and in-house, or you are renting your own crown jewel.`,
                    coach_r1_build_skip:    `<strong>Skipping the 800V platform forfeits the EV window.</strong> This is the growth race you exist to compete in; calling it peripheral is the closed-innovation reflex's mirror image — surrender.`,
                    coach_r1_thermal_build: `<strong>Building thermal-management from scratch is too slow.</strong> You lack the capability and the German rival ships in 9 months. This is a gap to source in, not to build over 18+ months.`,
                    coach_r1_thermal_ignore: `<strong>Ignoring the thermal-management gap is wishful.</strong> It is a real adjacency customers will notice. The open question is how to source it — ally or equity — not whether to.`,
                    coach_r1_closedreflex:  `<strong>That is the closed-innovation reflex.</strong> Invent everything, patent it, defend it — and leave money on the table while you get out-developed where you lack scale. Open innovation manages flows both ways.`,
                    coach_r1_grid_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp portfolio map.</strong> SiC to leverage out, the 800V platform kept closed and core, the thermal gap sourced in. That grid — centrality vs capability — is exactly what should drive every later decision.`,
                    coach_r2_carveout:      `<strong>Field-of-use design done right.</strong> Carving out robotics and e-mobility lets you bank the €9M while keeping your own lanes closed. You monetise the asset without arming a rival in your backyard.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>A broad licence with no carve-out arms a competitor.</strong> Eighteen months on, your licensee is selling SiC-controlled inverters into robotics — your fastest-growing segment — using your architecture. You banked cash and built a rival in your own lane.`,
                    coach_r2_narrow:        `<strong>A hyper-narrow licence nearly kills the deal.</strong> Protecting everything leaves almost nothing to license; you have designed the value out of the transaction. Carve out your lanes, not the entire field.`,
                    coach_r2_exclusive:     `<strong>A worldwide exclusive hands over a monopoly on your architecture.</strong> You lose optionality and freedom to operate. Non-exclusive with a royalty floor banks the base revenue and keeps your roadmap open.`,
                    coach_r2_leverage:      `<strong>This is technology leverage.</strong> A protected, non-exclusive licence with a royalty floor — and the cash ring-fenced to fund the inbound moves. Outbound financing inbound is exactly Frattini's point.`,
                    coach_r2_decline:       `<strong>You kept the SiC IP closed and idle.</strong> It is generating exactly what it generated last year: nothing. The €9M is off the table, the patents have less life left, and you will fund the build out of margin instead.`,
                    coach_r3_equity:        `<strong>Minority equity + access is the disciplined option.</strong> A staged real option on the thermal tech — cheaper than acquisition, reversible, and it buys you access and speed without busting the cap.`,
                    coach_r3_alliance:      `<strong>The alliance is fast and cheap but thin on control.</strong> €6M buys quick access; just be sure the IP governance is tight, because shared development is where know-how leaks.`,
                    coach_r3_acquire:       `<strong>The €30M acquisition blows the €24M cap.</strong> Full ownership, yes — but you have nothing left for the 800V build or any contingency. Did you need to own the technology, or just access it? A €11M equity-plus-access stake would have preserved both.`,
                    coach_r3_makeit:        `<strong>Building thermal-management yourself misses the window.</strong> You lack the capability and the rival ships in 9 months. Make is the wrong mode for a gap you cannot close fast — ally or take equity.`,
                    coach_r3_governed:      `<strong>Equity stake plus strong IP governance — well sequenced.</strong> Joint-IP clauses and background-IP firewalls let you open the boundary without leaking the know-how that makes you defensible.`,
                    coach_r3_leak:          `<strong>Light governance on a partnership leaks your IP.</strong> A handshake and an NDA, and you have shared inverter-control know-how to make it work. When the spin-out's next round is led by your German rival, who owns the joint IP?`,
                    coach_r3_ungoverned:    `<strong>You took a partner but governed nothing.</strong> Inbound openness without IP-ownership clauses and firewalls teaches a partner you do not control. Govern the boundary or do not open it.`,
                    coach_r3_slow:          `<strong>Ignoring the clock is costly.</strong> Time-to-market is first-order here: a platform that arrives 18 months out is worth far less than access available in 9. Sequence for speed against the rival.`,
